    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The strongest libertarian justification relies on the moral philosophy of John Locke, positing that property is an extension of one's own person derived from labor, making theft (which includes taxation without direct reciprocation) a fundamental violation of self-ownership. Libertarians argue that any redistribution scheme inherently treats individuals as mere components of a greater machine, thereby crushing the very agency that defines human freedom. By enforcing these absolute property rights, they contend that the state must step entirely out of the realm of social welfare to avoid becoming a \"night-watchman\" that arbitrarily decides who survives and who does not. This framework asserts that protecting the right to keep the fruits of one's labor is the only moral obligation because no individual has the right to claim another's freedom merely to achieve a greater societal good.",
